    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "We've looked for mold or grease, but we've wiped the fabric down with industrial cleaners and even run a test batch of a completely different material, and the same thing happened. The voids are just... there. They aren't smears, they aren't stains, and they aren't anything we can see with the naked eye before the press comes up. \n\nIt's driving the crew crazy because it feels like we're playing a trick on ourselves. It's so random. One print is perfect, the next one has these little \"holes\" in the design. If the sensors aren't catching any drops in temp or pressure, and the chemicals are clean, what could possibly be causing a piece of ink to just fail to exist in a specific spot? Is there any concept in physics or manufacturing for something that just \"drops out\" of a process like that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
